      "sequence_position": 1,
      "problem": "If ${log}_{36}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2},$ then what is $x?$",
      "worked_solution": "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $36,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{36}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {36}^{{log}_{36}⁡x} & ={36}^{1/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${36}^{{log}_{36}⁡x}=x.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={36}^{1/2} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{36} \\\\ x & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$",
